 model of the human brain provides  us with a model of the human brain and  what I'm showing you on this slide is a  rendering to scale of two MRI images of  the rhesus macaque monkey on the left  and the human  brain on the right what's important  about these mr. this is this image is  that the monkey brain is only two and a  half times smaller in each dimension  than that of the human approximately  that gives us about a 10 to 15 factor 10  to 15 difference in size and this is  captured by the weights of the two  brains the numbers of neurons in the  brains and the number of neurons in the  cortex of each of these brains and so  this is a really important image for us  because it shows how similar the monkey  brain is to the human brain and allows  us to develop our technologies in the  monkey in a way that can be directly  translated to humans another really  important aspect to our work is the  nature of the signals that we're  recording and analyzing and this slide  what I'm showing you are three different  recording technologies that are  currently used to study the human brain  the EEG which is shown and measured at  the scalp with a one centimeter size  electrode energy which is measured using  a special sensor which is outside the  scalp and even further away they  measures magnetic fields and inside the  brain with the invasive measurements  available in a select population for  clinical purposes we can measure the  electric cortical gram or ACOG signal  and that signal is made at the surface  of the brain under the dura and with an  electrode that's about four millimeters  in size so the technologies that allow  these measurements invasively within the  brain are shown on the right  they are the subdural cortical electrode  which is shown in the top left panel  it's a grid and the depth electrodes  which penetrate into the brain and are  shown below and each one of these probes  has a specificity of around many  millimeters and space by typically  something like a centimeter when we  consider the ability to record  near liked to be more directly we can  need to think about the micro recordings  and so now what I'm sure  instead of recording types you can make  by going into the brain at a finer scale  so I'm showing you the micro aqaq which  is measured at the same location as the  E [ __ ] signal but with a recording  electrode that's only a hundred microns  and then we have the laminar electrodes  in the micro electrode and the only  federally approved device that can  perform these kinds of invasive  recordings clinically is the Utah right  then that records spiking and fill  potentials so there's a big gap between  what we can do  invasively at the micro scale and this a  more macro ukog scale so what I want to  talk about today is how we can bridge  this gap and I'll start by talking about  how to interpret the measurements we can  make before moving on to considering the  computations performed by the brain and  how we can analyze them and then how we  can translate the new technologies from  monkeys to humans so interpreting  large-scale brain dynamics using field  potentials which is how we record the  signals I've just talked about depends  on having a model for understanding the  measurement the biophysical forward  model is the model which generates from  the source signals in the brain the  field potentials that we can measure and  that by physical model is informed by  our understanding of cellular biophysics  and electricity magnetism in physics so  we have different types of cells and we  can record the activity on the axons and  dendrites axon shown in red dendrite  shown in blue and what's important about  this model is that it teaches us that  when we have for example these stellate  cells which are found in layer 4 they  have large axonal dendrites excuse me  external extents but small dendritic  extents and this type of geometry gives  rise to a small field potential in  response to synaptic input in contrast  the other type of neuron that I'm  showing you here at cortical neuron the  layer 5 pyramid generates large  potentials because the axons are not  weighted so much by the foot potential  measurement and the dendrites are  extended and this is what creates a  large signal that we can measure with an  now the biophysical Ford model isn't the  most important Ford model to our work  what we also care about is how  computations are being performed and so  to understand that we have to consider  what we call a functional Ford model so  the functional Ford model considers the  actual selectivity of neural activity in  the brain and how that gives rise to  selectivity in our measurements and so  what we have here are two examples of  selectivity which is organized on the  left with the arrows showing the spatial  coding of a variable involved in  behavior that smoothly changes as you  move across the cortical surface and the  measurements we make for field  potentials in those situations would  pool across these neighboring  measurement signals and give rise to a  large amplitude response in the  functional Ford model while if the  signals are not smoothly varying in the  selectivity across the sheet then we  would get cancellation effectively of  the signals and small amplitude  selectivity so an EEG signal might give  us the video alone are measurements  because it a verge of or large enough  regions of the brain that the topography  is lost while a micro eco measurement or  a field potential a local field  potential measurement measured more  close to the source more selectively may  give us a larger amplitude signal so  these ideas are how we think about the  interpretation of the philodendron  measurements now the computations that  we're interested in are informed by our  understanding of behavior and how the  brain controls behavior in the animal  model and the monkeys we've been  studying the visually guided behaviors  and this is illustrated here where we  have vision entering the brain guiding  actions and then being used in this case  to guide looking movement by movements  and arm movements conceptually the way  we analyze these computations is in  terms of sensory and motor maps we think  of the eyes as being seeing the world  around us and the sensory cues in that  world and when those cues are to  instruct actions then we need to  transform those sensory cues intermodal  commands or intended movements and so  the transformation that we're interested  in goes from the sensory map to the  motor map and in the case of an eye  movement we can think of this quite  literally as the map of the world where  there are stimuli in it and then the  need to move the eyes to acquire those  stimuli the kinds of behavioral tasks  that we can use so study this our  correspondingly quite simple we have a  monkey staring at a screen he has a cue  that he remembers the location of  following short delay he moved his eyes  to that remembered location and we find  that neurons in the brain fire rapidly  and in response to the presentation of  the cue throughout the delay and then  turn off after the movement as we vary  where the cues presented in space we  find different types of responses from  the same neuron in this example this  neuron prefers or encodes space down and  to the right of where when he's looking  and the other locations are not as well  encoded by the neurons activity now  these neuronal signals are quite hard to  record and impede which impedes our  ability to translate technologies based  on their activity work done in my lab  and earlier has shown that field  potentials the field potentials we've  just been talking about also contains  detailed information about the processes  involving sensory motor transformations  here I'm showing you two spectrograms  which show us the power in the signal as  the monkey is doing the task with the  cue onset time being shown at C the  psychotic eye movement being shown at s  and we can see that as we move through  time and frequency there are ranges of  power in frequency ranges of power and  frequencies between 30 to 80 Hertz which  are increased when the monkey wants to  look to what we call the preferred  duration for that site compared to the  anti preferred direction when we  quantify the selectivity of the field  potentials and compare it with the  selectivity of the firing rate of  neurons we see that there  very similar the firing rate of the  neuron and the potential power R  similarly tuned so this shows that we  can analyze neural computations involved  with sensory motor behaviors using field  potentials instead of only recording  neurons so how can we apply this to  study functions of the human brain that  are unique  well speech is uniquely human faculty  and we've been in discussion with them  in the field there is this idea that the  similar computational mechanisms support  speech as these other sensory motor  behaviors and so david poeppel a pioneer  in neurolinguistics  advised me to study speech as if a  monkey could talk and the reason for  this is the in a model that he and his  colleague Greg Hickok have developed  these sensory motor transformations for  speech are organized in the brain  similarly to that of the other sensory  motor transformations in animals and so  this diagram just shows the range of  interactions across the two different  hemispheres in the human brain  supporting speech processing  conceptually the idea remains the same  computational idea remains the same we  have a perceptual map for speech it gets  transformed into a motor map that we use  to make an utterance to speak and the  relationship between these two maps and  how the computations connect them to go  from sensation to action is the type of  computation that we can analyze based on  work in animals and so the strategy for  studying speeches of the monkey to talk  is to present stimuli that the animal  hears in this case the human hears and  then speaks based on in this case just  repeats and so the task is to listen to  the sound and then repeat the word that  you hear you can also have a condition  where you just listen to the sound  without speaking so that you can hear it  but just to mime it and there's a listen  speaking listen mind conditions are  really important for isolating sensory  motor processes processes relate to  sensation and action and not just  sensation or action we performed a set  of studies to record the  acog signal in human subjects epilepsy  patients who are having electrodes  implanted for clinical purposes and we  find that we can see sites in the brain  which respond to the movement to utter  really respective of whether or not the  subjects making any noise they're not  speaking they're just moving their mouth  we also get other responses which are  selected for hearing the sounds but not  speaking the sounds they can also hear  themselves speak in the left panel and  then we have these sensory motor  responses which connect sensation in  action by responding both to the hearing  and the speech for each condition even  when miming and so this set of results  pointed to the existence of a sensory  motor speech system that linked  perceptual inputs and motor outputs and  we've hypothesized that along with  others that this interface the sensory  motor interface supports access to the  linguistic processes which we need to  ascribe meaning to our speech the  results associated with this study were  quite important because they showed in  fact that these responses were bilateral  that is to say they were found in both  the left and the right hemisphere which  was not expected based on the work of  hikakin poeple and others indicating  that our understanding of how this level  of speech processing is lateralized is  incomplete and points are the kinds of  new findings we can make about the  organization of neural computations in  the human brain using fill potentials so  now I want to move on to our ability to  analyze these neural computations using  next-generation devices in particular I  want to talk about so-called flexible  printed circuit board tech based  technologies and the reason why is  because they're really powerful for  translational goals  these devices are microfabricated  they're cheap and low-cost they're  available for manufacturing commercially  they are also able to be used to make  biocompatible implants and critically  since they're based on printed circuit  boards we can  great more advanced electronic circuits  that make the devices wireless and smart  so we've done a number of studies to  test these devices in our monkeys the  way we've done this is to revise how we  think about the testing cycle instead of  pairing and training animals and then  implanting a device and seeing how it  does revising the device and then  implanting it again in a different  animal we developed a system whereby we  could package the device shown in panel  E and then place the inside and implants  shown in panel D and F that allows us to  prepare one animal and then implant many  devices rapidly this is because we don't  need to re implant-- the animal  surgically we can do it while they're  awake we've also used the system to  record wirelessly overnight and here I'm  showing you an ode what we call it's a K  each system that has a wireless antennas  on it and then we added a wireless  transmitter to the previous assembly  that I showed you what recording these  signals overnight allowed us to do is to  study for the first time these signals  in naturally sleeping monkeys and what  we can do is look at the sleep dynamics  so this is a different kind of activity  than what we've been talking about but  illustrates the power of this approach  of recording the micro eco signals on  those electrodes and so in this slide  what I'm showing you are the different  types of phenomena that we've observed  where we see signals which are present  both on the spiking of the neurons the  field potentials inside the brain and on  the II cog near and far and that's in  the global example it's present on all  the contacts we recorded from in this  experiment at the same time we also have  ever see other kinds of responses which  are local which is to say they're on the  II cog and they're on the LFP but not  they're not on all the e cogs and  channels there's a blue the far ACOG is  silent in this case during the event we  can also find other events which  more found on the field potentials then  on either of the e [ __ ] signals and  finally we can see events that propagate  across the cortical sheep and in fact  from the depths of the brain to the  surface of the brain all of this is to  show how well the micro eco seconds can  whistle resolve high resolution signals  in sleeping brain which is previously  only been studied using more macroscopic  measurements like EEG that do not  resolve these different kinds of  responses most recently we've been  taking these devices and testing them in  patients with epilepsy again but instead  of recording the e cog signal we're  recording the micro eco Google using our  new device here I'm showing you  intraoperative recordings of the micro  eco signal during seizure related events  and this shows about a hundred of the  electrodes that we're recording from and  you can see as you go down through these  different electrodes which are spaced  quite closely the activity is changing  across these different contacts quite  dramatically  you see events coming and going as you  move across the sheet and now I want to  show you a video of this data the same  data that I'm showing you in the time  series but now as a movie and you'll see  the different colors showing the  different voltages of the signal over  time  you  so what I hope you can see in that movie  is the the activity across the all the  different contacts is swirling and very  dynamic and rich with structure in ways  that we could not have observed if we  had not made the measurement the way  that we did that is to say with high  resolution using the micro electrode  array and this is really important for  clinical applications because the  different types of events need to be  diagnosed differently and the treatment  that the patients will receive should  reflect this diagnosis then in  conclusion I've told you about how Phil  potentials can be used to interpret and  understand the organization of brain  activity and the functional forward  model is a very important concept for  this because it allows us to interpret  the selectivity in the signals and how  we use them to understand neural  computations I've also shown you how we  can develop a framework for analyzing  your computations that's been used to  study visually guided behaviors and  animals and to do so for what is a  uniquely human faculty that of speech  I've presented how we can translate new  technologies that are low cost and high  resolution that will allow us to resolve  dynamics in more detailed ways than we  could before and offer new treatment  paradigms we hope thank you very much  thank you dr. Peston for that excellent  talk as a reminder our speaker will  follow up any questions about his  presentation by email  continuing with our innovative  neurotechnologies track we have the  outstanding dr. Georges Ozaki presenting  next Doctor Who sakis top title is high  resolution recording and non-invasive  perturbation tools for the human brain  hope to see you there

